 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6050+) **Large AI armies parked with nothing reachable to attack:** BBAI logging showed a major Base AdvCiv/K-Mod bug: even while at peace, with no planned war and no rival or Barbarian city it could reach, the AI could keep waiting for reinforcements and effectively lock more units into the same army so they could not be released for other duties. For example, in save file 450, a United States group grew from 6 units on T126 to 18 of the empire's 47 military units by T225, and then, after the short war, it returned to peace still holding 21 of 51 units on T247. So it concentrated about 40% of the AI's military in one city and left far fewer units available to defend the rest of its empire or pursue another opportunity, which was very problematic. To fix this, when the AI has nothing to attack, it now stops waiting for more reinforcements: if the army is already in its own territory and not immediately threatened, the AI now only keeps one modest core group ready for a future city attack. As for the excess units, they now instead leave the group and resume defending cities or preparing another expedition. Note: an army that is still abroad or in immediate danger remains together until it returns to its own territory and is no longer immediately threatened, so the fix does not break it apart while exposed. Note 2: the units can also regroup into a large army as soon as a real target or planned war appears. After the fix, confirming runs of save files 450 and 452 showed the intended behavior: in save file 452, Japan reduced its peacetime city-assault group with nothing to attack from 21 to 11 units, freeing 10 units for other duties; at the same time, the AI could still assemble large armies when a real war began, as Carthage attacked Susa with 63 units in save file 450, and China attacked Aksum with roughly 180-202 units in save file 452. See [KI#188.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#1883---fixedimproved-major-base-advcivk-mod-bug-city-assault-armies-without-a-reachable-target-or-war-plan-could-keep-waiting-for-reinforcements-indefinitely-and-park-40-53-of-an-ais-military).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6051+) **AI war preparations no longer repeatedly gamble away their own mobilization:** BBAI logging in save file 452 showed a major UWAI issue: across all AIs, 152 war preparations began, 130 were canceled or redirected to another target, and only 17 became wars. Whenever UWAI reviewed a preparation whose war value had become negative, it converted how doubtful the plan looked into a chance to abandon it and rolled that chance on every negative review. China alone began 36 preparations: repeated abandonment rolls canceled 19, separate target-switch rolls redirected another 13, and only 2 became wars. Established preparations also used a random roll to decide when to become direct war. This could waste the military advantage and upkeep already committed to mobilization: 32 of 74 cancellations even selected the exact same target again later in the same team turn. To fix this, the AI now keeps a newly chosen plan through its first two reviews unless a hard failure, such as an illegal target or expired unprofitable preparation, requires immediate cancellation. After that, it cancels only when the personality- and game-speed-adjusted evidence reaches the tunable maximum for a clearly bad plan. It switches only when another rival is clearly better, uses a stable readiness threshold before beginning direct war, and cannot cancel and recreate the same target during one team turn; other targets remain available throughout. In the final confirming save-file 452 run, cancellations fell from 74 to 34, target switches from 22 to 15, same-turn reselections from 32 to 0, and reselections within three turns from 41 to 5, while actual wars increased from 22 to 29. Save file 450 independently confirmed no same-turn reselection, 39 preparations advancing to direct war, and 35 wars starting. See [KI#189](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#189---fixedimproved-major-uwai-issue-repeated-random-reviews-could-cancel-or-redirect-most-war-preparations-before-they-became-wars).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6051+) **War preparations against near-winning rivals no longer forget why the rival was chosen:** the same investigation found an AdvCiv-SAS integration bug in the victory-denial improvements from KI#184. China selected India on T237 with a war value of 320, including 300 because India threatened victory, but the inherited next-turn review omitted that urgency, saw only 17, and switched away. Reviews and alternative-target comparisons now preserve the victory-denial value; if an existing preparation later meets the same narrow distance, power, naval, and victory-timing limits used for emergency war selection, the AI can declare immediately instead of losing more turns. In the independent save-file 450 confirmation, this later direct-war path fired three times and all three declarations appeared in the game history, including wars against near-Space-victory rivals on T255 and T281. See [KI#189](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#189---fixedimproved-major-uwai-issue-repeated-random-reviews-could-cancel-or-redirect-most-war-preparations-before-they-became-wars) and [KI#184](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#184---fixedimproved-multiple-uwai-victory-denial-issues-could-let-imminent-space-winners-survive-no-war-before-fixing-this-regardless-of-whether-ai-was-very-strong-militarily-like-in-the-ramesses-run-or-very-weak-like-in-the-lincoln-run-then-even-after-the-initial-fix-wars-were-still-too-late-too-short-or-too-low-impact).
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6056+) **When the AI decides to prepare a war, it now chooses the rival it considers best instead of gambling on worse alternatives:** Base AdvCiv UWAI rolled separately for every eligible rival in ranked order. If its preferred target failed the random roll, it could then prepare against a much less valuable rival whose separate roll happened to succeed. For example, on turn 215 of save file 450, China rejected nearby Ethiopia despite rating it at 68% war drive, rejected England at 7%, and then selected faraway India at only 5%. To fix this, the AI now only makes one personality-based roll for its highest final-drive target: if it fails, the AI prepares no war that turn instead of attacking someone it considers worse. In the confirming save-file 450 and 452 runs, all 81 successful selections chose the highest final-drive target, while warfare remained active. This behavior is XML-tunable and can be disabled to restore the inherited independent rolls. See [KI#191](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#191---fixedimproved-base-advciv-uwai-issue-independent-target-rolls-could-reject-the-best-war-target-and-then-select-a-much-worse-rival).
